    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Mohenjo-daro was built around 2500 BCE as a city with a planned layout on a grid, and it was one of the largest settlements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, with an estimated population of at least 40,000.</li><li>It had more than 700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, covered drains along its main streets and a large public bath, but no evidence shows that priests or monarchs ruled it, and its original name is unknown.</li><li>It was abandoned by about 1700 BCE, remained undocumented for around 3,700 years, and was identified by R. D. Banerji in 1919 and 1920 before excavations from 1924 revealed its plan.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Excavations have found more than 700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> at Mohenjo-daro, about one well for every three houses, a number that is unheard of when compared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> at the time.</p></aside>
    </section>

