    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Mohenjo-daro is built on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> plain as one of the large cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1700 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> civilisation goes into sudden decline and Mohenjo-daro is abandoned</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">R. D. Banerji visits the site, finds a flint scraper and is convinced of its antiquity</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text">Large-scale excavations begin under Kashinath Dikshit, followed by John Marshall in 1925</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1926</span><span class="rf-text">The bronze statuette known as the Dancing Girl is found, and the soapstone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priest-king">Priest-King</a> follows in 1927</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1965</span><span class="rf-text">The last major excavations, led by George Dales, end and further excavation is banned because of weathering damage</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1980</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> designates the site a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> and an agreement to fund restoration is made</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harappa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harappa.jpg" alt="Harappa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Granary_and_Great_Hall_on_Mound_F_-_Archaeological_site_of_Harappa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Muhammad Bin Naveed, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mohenjo-daro stands off the right (west) bank of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> river in Larkana District, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, about 28 kilometres from the town of Larkana. It lies on a Pleistocene ridge in the flood plain of the Indus. The city was built around 2500 BCE within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, also called the Harappan Civilisation, which developed around 3000 BCE from the prehistoric Indus culture. At its height that civilisation spanned much of what is now Pakistan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-india">North India</a>, reaching west to the Iranian border, south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and north to an outpost in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, with major urban centres at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a>, Mohenjo-daro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothal">Lothal</a>, Kalibangan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dholavira">Dholavira</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakhigarhi">Rakhigarhi</a>.</p><p>The city's original name is unknown. From his analysis of a seal found there, Iravatham Mahadevan speculated that the ancient name could have been Kukkuṭārma, "the city of the cockerel". Mohenjo-daro is the modern name, interpreted in Sindhi as "Mound of the Dead". The site was built in a relatively short period, and the water supply system and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> were among its first planned constructions. Its layout was planned, with rectilinear buildings arranged on a grid, and most were built of fired and mortared brick.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mohenjo-daro was one of the largest cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, and the covered area is estimated at 300 hectares. Its population is estimated at at least 40,000; the Oxford Handbook of Cities in World History offers a "weak" estimate of a peak population of around 40,000. The city prospered for several centuries. Its size, and its provision of public buildings and facilities, suggest a high level of social organisation.</p><p>The city was divided into two parts, the Citadel and the Lower City. The Citadel, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> mound around 12 metres high, supported public baths, a large residential structure designed to house about 5,000 citizens and two large assembly halls. Some buildings combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fired-brick">fired brick</a> with sun-dried mud-brick and wooden superstructures, and some had two storeys.</p><p>Excavation has recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>, carved seals, balance-scales and weights, gold and jasper jewellery, and bronze and copper figurines and bowls made by the lost wax technique. Furnaces on the site were used for copperworking and melting metals rather than smelting. A section in the north-eastern part of the site seems to have been dedicated to shell-working. Copper tablets carry examples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-script">Indus script</a>, which has not been deciphered.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/priest-king"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/priest-king.jpg" alt="Priest-King" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/priest-king">Priest-King</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohenjo-daro_Priesterk%C3%B6nig.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mamoon Mengal, CC BY-SA 1.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nothing is known of the names of Mohenjo-daro's rulers. There is no evidence that priests or monarchs ruled the city, although archaeologists gave the name "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priest-king">Priest-King</a>" to a seated soapstone figure found in 1927 in a building with unusually ornamental brickwork and a wall-niche. The figure is 17.5 centimetres tall and shows a neatly bearded man with pierced earlobes, a fillet around his head, an armband and a cloak with trefoil and circle motifs.</p><p>The city's size and public buildings are taken to point to a high level of social organisation, and the combination of potentially religious and administrative buildings frames Mohenjo-daro as a developed urban city. Scholars have thought the so-called College Hall a priestly residence, and a small private structure with eight bathrooms north of the Great Bath is believed to have been reserved for priests who lived on the level above.</p><p>From the fortifications and from the structure of other major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harappa">Harappa</a>, scholars have postulated that Mohenjo-daro was an administrative centre. Harappa and Mohenjo-daro share a similar layout and were generally not as heavily fortified as other Indus sites. The identical city layouts of all Indus sites show that there was some kind of political or administrative centrality, but its extent and functioning remain unclear.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mortimer-wheeler"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mortimer-wheeler.jpg" alt="Mortimer Wheeler" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mortimer-wheeler">Mortimer Wheeler</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mortimer_Wheeler_in_1939.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bassano Ltd, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most houses had inner courtyards, with doors that opened onto side lanes. Individual households or groups of households drew water from smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, and a large central well, with a doorway onto the main street, served the general public. Covered drains lined the major streets and most likely carried waste towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. Some houses, presumably those of more prestigious inhabitants, had rooms set aside for bathing, and one building had an underground furnace, possibly for heated bathing.</p><p>Near the Citadel stood a large public bath, the "Great Bath", where steps led from a colonnaded courtyard down to a brick pool lined with bitumen, 12 metres long, 7 metres wide and 2.4 metres deep; it may have been used for religious purification. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mortimer-wheeler">Mortimer Wheeler</a> identified a nearby building as a "Great Granary", but Jonathan Mark Kenoyer noted the complete lack of evidence for grain there and argued that it might better be called a "Great Hall" of uncertain function.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">Ivory</a> rulers were divided into units of about 34 millimetres with decimal subdivisions, and ancient bricks in the region have dimensions that correspond to these units. Terracotta figurines of women were found, some showing domestic activities and toys, others pregnancy, believed to be offerings for fertility. A seal shows a seated figure among animals, taken by some scholars as a yogi and by others as a "proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>". Cock-fighting may have had ritual and religious significance.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mohenjo-daro&#8221; cites where it covers Architecture and urban infrastructure, Major buildings, Water supply and wells:</p><ul><li>Mortimer Wheeler, The Indus Civilization (Cambridge University Press, 1960)</li><li>Rita P. Wright, The ancient Indus : urbanism, economy, and society (Cambridge University Press, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city was repeatedly threatened by flooding. Large platforms, perhaps intended as defence against floods, were raised during periods of reconstruction to lift the buildings. According to a theory first advanced by Wheeler, the city could have been flooded and silted over perhaps six times and rebuilt each time in the same place. Some archaeologists believed that the site was abandoned after a final flood engulfed it in mud.</p><p>Gregory Possehl was the first to theorise that the floods were caused by overuse and expansion upon the land, and that a mud flood was not the reason the site was abandoned. He proposed constant minor floods through the year, together with land worn out by crops, pastures and the resources used for bricks and pottery.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> civilisation went into sudden decline around 1700 BCE, and Mohenjo-daro was abandoned by that time, along with other large cities of the civilisation. Archaeologists have identified the foundations of eroded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> walls that once surrounded the city, guard towers to the west of the main settlement and defensive fortifications to the south. The ruins then remained undocumented for around 3,700 years. A Buddhist stupa of 150 to 500 CE stood on the mound.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>R. D. Banerji of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> visited the site in 1919 and 1920, looking for the Buddhist stupa known to be there, and a flint scraper convinced him of its antiquity. Large-scale excavations followed under Kashinath Dikshit in 1924 and 1925 and John Marshall in 1925 and 1926, then in the 1930s, in 1945 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mortimer-wheeler">Mortimer Wheeler</a> and in 1964 and 1965 under George Dales. After 1965 excavation was banned because of weathering damage to the exposed structures. Core drilling in 2015 showed that the site is larger than the unearthed area.</p><p>The finds were first deposited in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> Museum and later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">Partition of India</a> about 12,000 objects, mostly sherds of pottery, were split equally between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Pakistan received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priest-king">Priest-King</a>, India the Dancing Girl and the Pashupati seal, and a small group of artefacts went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> in 1939.</p><p>The site was designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1980, the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>, and it is threatened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a> salinity and improper restoration. In 2012 Pakistani archaeologists warned that without better conservation it could disappear by 2030.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mohenjo-daro was built around 2500 BCE on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> as one of the largest cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, with a planned grid, a Citadel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and drains. It was abandoned by about 1700 BCE and is now an archaeological site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>.</em></p>

